[dvystrcil/homelab] Monthly test restore of restic backup for claude memory  (model=qwen3-coder-next:latest, 48s, lane=claude)
## Why
The Claude Code memory is stored in a restic backup, but there is no automated verification that the backup can be successfully restored. Without periodic test restores, a silent backup failure could go unnoticed until an actual recovery is needed — risking loss of valuable memory state.

## What we know
- The homelab uses restic for backups; a live pod `etcd-backup-29900280-gv5h5` shows scheduled backup jobs are already running (source: LIVE PODS). - https://github.com/dvystrcil/homelab/issues/1911 TrueNAS replication failures produce only email alerts, not issues — similar pattern of silent failures (source: CANDIDATE EXISTING ISSUES).
- https://github.com/dvystrcil/homelab/issues/1603 discusses NFS archiveOnDelete and expiring archived-* dirs after N days, indicating existing archival logic exists in the homelab (source: CANDIDATE EXISTING ISSUES).
- The homelab already has scheduled jobs for other services (e.g., pihole-backup, vaultwarden-backup) implemented as CronJobs (source: LIVE PODS).
- The homelab uses ArgoCD for GitOps; new CronJob manifests can be added to the cluster config under `homelab/cluster/` (source: architecture docs and repo structure).

**unverified**: exact path to the restic repository and credentials for claude memory backup; exact command syntax for `restic restore --test` or equivalent dry-run verification.

## Acceptance criteria
- [ ] **AC1.** A monthly CronJob runs `restic restore --target /tmp/test-restore` (or equivalent dry-run/verification command) and exits non-zero on failure.
- [ ] **AC2.** The job is visible in `kubectl get cronjobs -A` and its history is recorded in `kubectl get jobs`.
- [ ] **AC3.** On failure, the job triggers an issue via the Health Event Router (per https://github.com/dvystrcil/homelab/issues/1925) or sends an alert to the ops channel.

## Lane
claude — The backup location, credentials, and exact restic verification method are unverified and require design decisions (e.g., where to mount the repo, how to inject secrets, whether to use `restic check` vs `restore --dry-run`).

